  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The recommended and most scalable choice is <b>\"Configure a deployment using CloudFormation StackSet, targeting the specified Organizational Unit and declaring the necessary AWS Regions for rollout.\"</b> CloudFormation StackSets let you propagate a CloudFormation stack simultaneously across multiple AWS accounts and regions, and you can specify an entire OU as the deployment target. This ensures consistency and efficient governance for managing resources like AWS Config rules at organizational scale.<br><br><i>\"Use CodePipeline to create a pipeline where a source stage uploads the CloudFormation template and a deploy stage launches the stack in every account found in the OU.\"</i> is incorrect because CodePipeline alone doesn't natively handle multi-account, multi-region deployment targeting OUs—it's designed for CI/CD pipelines and requires custom scripting for this use case.<br><br><i>\"Set up an IAM role with permissions to manage AWS Config rules within the OU, then utilize this role to run a CodeDeploy process that delivers CloudFormation stacks to each account.\"</i> is incorrect, as CodeDeploy is not suitable for managing CloudFormation stack deployments, and IAM roles by themselves do not automate stack rollout across multiple accounts.<br><br><i>\"Include parameters for AWS account IDs and regions in your CloudFormation template, and use the root account to deploy the stack into all accounts within the OU.\"</i> is not best practice and is error-prone for large organizations; it doesn't scale well and increases the risk of deployment failures due to manual steps.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Always leverage StackSets for organization-wide resource stack deployments, as it centralizes management and supports automatic updates for accounts added to the specified OU.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You work as a DevOps engineer at an organization that uses AWS Organizations to manage many AWS accounts, grouped by Organizational Units (OUs). You need to roll out a CloudFormation stack to configure custom AWS Config rules across every account in a specific OU. What is the most efficient way to deploy this stack to all required accounts and regions?</p>",
